Congratulations! You have completed this module. At this point, you understand that:

AI agents are autonomous systems that perceive their environment, reason about goals, and take actions to achieve desired outcomes.

Not every problem requires an AI agent—simple LLM calls or predefined workflows are often better suited for predictable or low-risk tasks.

AI agents are most effective for complex tasks that require flexibility, decision-making, and interaction with external tools or systems.

AI agents differ from traditional AI systems by combining reasoning, memory, and tool use within an iterative perceive–reason–act cycle.

Tool calling enables LLMs to access real-time data, external APIs, databases, and code execution, allowing them to move beyond guessing and take real-world actions.

Traditional tool calling can introduce errors or hallucinations, while embedded tool calling through libraries or frameworks can improve reliability and execution.

AI agents have important limitations, including reliability concerns, higher computational costs, and the need for human oversight.

Effective agent design involves clear task boundaries, thoughtful tool definitions, and monitoring to reduce risk and improve accuracy.

AI agents can be built either from scratch or by using frameworks, each approach offering different trade-offs in control, complexity, and development speed.

Building a simple AI agent from scratch provides a strong foundation for understanding more advanced agent architectures and frameworks introduced in later modules.
